<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cinnamoyl-CoA biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6457" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CINNAMOYL-COA Cinnamoyl-CoA| and the closely related compound, |FRAME: P-COUMAROYL-COA (&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-4-coumaroyl-CoA| are important precursors to a number of compound classes, including phenylpropanoids, flavonoids, and volatile benzenoids in plants and polyketides in plants and bacteria |CITS: [12502351][19258320]|. Members of these classes can play many roles, for instance as defense, signaling, pigment, and aroma compounds. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Both |FRAME: CINNAMOYL-COA cinnamoyl-CoA| and |FRAME: P-COUMAROYL-COA (&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-4-coumaroyl-CoA| are derived from |FRAME: CPD-674 cinnamate| produced from |FRAME: PHE L-phenylalanine| by the activity of a phenylalanine ammonia lysase (PAL). Their only structural difference is the presence of a hydroxyl group in the 4-position on the benzene ring. If the hydroxyl group is added directly to |FRAME: CPD-674 cinnamate|, then |FRAME: P-COUMAROYL-COA (&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-4-coumaroyl-CoA| can be made without a |FRAME: CINNAMOYL-COA cinnamoyl-CoA| intermediate (see |FRAME: PWY1F-467 phenylpropanoid biosynthesis, initial reactions| and |FRAME: PWY-361 phenylpropanoid biosynthesis|). However, as shown in this pathway, |FRAME: CPD-674 cinnamate| can be also be a direct substrate for a CoA ligase. In some cases, the |FRAME: CINNAMOYL-COA cinnamoyl-CoA| generated by this enzyme may be used directly in secondary metabolic pathways (e.g. |FRAME: PWY-6443 benzoate biosynthesis I (CoA-dependent, &amp;beta;-oxidative)|, |FRAME: PWY-5059 pinobanksin biosynthesis|), however, it may also be used to produce |FRAME: P-COUMAROYL-COA (&lt;i&gt;E&lt;/i&gt;)-4-coumaroyl-CoA| through a late hydroxylation (e.g. |FRAME: PWY-6432 curcuminoid biosynthesis|). Although PAL has been well-characterized in plants since its initial discovery in 1961 |CITS: [14458851]|, it wasn&apos;t until much later than an enzyme believed to be responsible for catalyzing the second step in this pathway was first identified in bacteria |CITS: [12502351]|. In plants, the first step in this pathway is often rate limiting, for example, in the production of flavonoids. Therefore, expression levels and activity levels of PAL are often highly regulated and changes are induced by different stresses (rev. in |CITS: [17612622]|, ex. in |CITS: [16456646]|).</Description>
